        "title": "2. Gauss-Jordan Method for Matrix Inverse [A | I] ~ [I | A⁻¹]",
        "content": "### 1. The Gauss-Jordan Inversion Algorithm (Theoretical Analysis)\n\n> 📌 **University Exam Distinction:** Never use the classical Adjugate formula $A^{-1} = \\frac{\\text{adj}(A)}{|A|}$ when the question explicitly states **\"Find inverse using Gauss-Jordan method\"**! The classical method requires $O(n^4)$ or $O(n!)$ operations via minors, whereas Gauss-Jordan requires only $O(n^3)$ floating-point operations.\n\n#### Mathematical Principle of Gauss-Jordan Inversion:\nLet $A$ be an $n \\times n$ invertible matrix ($|A| ≠ 0$). If a sequence of elementary row operations $E_1, E_2, \..., E_k$ transforms $A$ into the identity matrix $I$:\n$$(E_k \... E_2 E_1) A = I ⇒ \\mathbf{A^{-1} = E_k \... E_2 E_1}$$\nTherefore, if we apply the identical sequence of operations to the augmented matrix $[ A \| I_n ]$:\n$$(E_k \... E_2 E_1) [ A \| I_n ] = [ (E_k \... E_1) A \| (E_k \... E_1) I_n ] = \\mathbf{[ I_n \| A^{-1} ]}$$\n\n---\n\n### 2. Step-by-Step Algorithmic Execution Pipeline\n\n1. **Augmentation:** Construct the partitioned block matrix $[ A \| I_n ]$ of dimension $n \\times 2n$.\n2. **Forward Elimination (Upper Triangularization):**\n   - For column $j = 1, 2, \..., n$:\n     - **Pivoting:** Find row $i ≥ j$ with the largest absolute pivot $|a_{ij}|$ to minimize floating-point round-off error. Swap $R_j \\leftrightarrow R_i$.\n     - If pivot entry $a_{jj} = 0$, matrix is **singular** ($A^{-1}$ does not exist!).\n     - Normalize the pivot row: $R_j \→ \\frac{1}{a_{jj}} R_j$.\n     - Eliminate all entries below pivot: for each $k > j$, $R_k \→ R_k - a_{kj} R_j$.\n3. **Backward Elimination (Diagonalization):**\n   - Starting from the bottom row $j = n, n-1, \..., 2$:\n     - For each row $k < j$, eliminate the above-pivot entries: $R_k \→ R_k - a_{kj} R_j$.\n4. **Extraction:** The right half of the augmented matrix is strictly $A^{-1}$. Verify that $A \· A^{-1} = I_n$.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Comparison of Matrix Inversion Techniques\n\n| Feature | Gauss-Jordan Method | Adjugate Formula | LU Decomposition |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Operation Count** | $n^3$ additions & multiplications | $\~ (n+1)!$ determinant operations | $\\frac{2}{3}n^3$ operations |\n| **Memory Required** | $n \\times 2n$ augmented grid | High cofactor matrix cache | $n \\times n$ in-place factorization |\n| **Suitability** | Hand calculation in university exams | $2 \\times 2$ matrices only | High-performance computer algorithms |\n\n---\n\n---\n\n\n**Problem:**\nFind the inverse of matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\end{bmatrix} using the Gauss-Jordan method.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Setup Augmented Matrix $[A \| I]$**\n$$[ A \mid I ] = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc|cc} 1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 0 & 1 \\end{array} \\right]$$\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Make entry below pivot zero ($R₂ \→ R₂ - 3R_1$)**\n$$\~ \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc|cc} 1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 & 1 \\end{array} \\right]$$\n\n**Step 3 🎯: Normalize pivot of row 2 ($R₂ \→ -\\frac{1}{2}R₂$)**\n$$\~ \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc|cc} 1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\end{array} \\right]$$\n\n**Step 4 🏁: Eliminate entry above pivot ($R₁ \→ R₁ - 2R_2$)**\n$$\~ \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc|cc} 1 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\end{array} \\right]$$\n\n\n🎯 Final Inverse: $\\mathbf{A^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix} -2 & 1 \\\\ 1.5 & -0.5 \\end{bmatrix}}$. Verified: $A \· A^{-1} = I$.\n\n\n\n\n**University Question (8 Marks):**\nFind A⁻¹ using Gauss-Jordan row operations for:\n$$A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$$\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Augmented Matrix [A \| I]**\n$$[A \mid I] = \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|ccc} 1 & 2 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{array} \\right]$$\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Backward Substitution Row Operations**\n- $R_2 \to R_2 - 3R_3$:\n$$\\sim \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|ccc} 1 & 2 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{array} \\right]$$\n- $R_1 \to R_1 - R_3$:\n$$\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|ccc} 1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{array} \\right]$$\n\n**Step 3 🎯: Eliminate entry above pivot $a_{22}=1$ ($R_1 \to R_1 - 2R_2$)**\n$$\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|ccc} 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{array} \\right]$$\n\n\n🎯 Resulting Inverse: $\\mathbf{A^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}}$.\n\n\n\n\n**University Question (10 Marks - MMU Question Paper):**\nEmploy the Gauss-Jordan method to determine the inverse of:\n$$A = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$$\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Swap Rows to Get Leading Pivot 1 (R₁ \\leftrightarrow R₂)**\n$$[A \mid I] = \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|ccc} 1 & 2 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{array} \\right]$$\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Eliminate Column 1 ($R_2 \to R_2 - 2R_1, \; R_3 \to R_3 - R_1$)**\n$$\~ \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|ccc} 1 & 2 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\end{array} \\right]$$\n\n**Step 3 🎯: Swap  \\leftrightarrow R_3$, Normalize and Eliminate**\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_2 &\\to -R_3: & \\left[0, 1, -1 \\mathrel{\\Big|} 0, 1, -1\\right] \\\\\nR_3 &\\to R_3 + 3R_2: & \\left[0, 0, -4 \\mathrel{\\Big|} 1, 1, -3\\right] \\\\\nR_3 &\\to -\\frac{1}{4}R_3: & \\left[0, 0, 1 \\mathrel{\\Big|} -\\frac{1}{4}, -\\frac{1}{4}, \\frac{3}{4}\\right]\n\\end{aligned}\n\n**Step 4 🏁: Eliminate Above Pivots to reach $[I \\mid A^{-1}]$**\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_2 &\\to R_2 + R_3: & \\left[0, 1, 0 \\mathrel{\\Big|} -\\frac{1}{4}, \\frac{3}{4}, -\\frac{1}{4}\\right] \\\\\nR_1 &\\to R_1 - R_3 - 2R_2: & \\left[1, 0, 0 \\mathrel{\\Big|} \\frac{3}{4}, -\\frac{1}{4}, -\\frac{1}{4}\\right]\n\\end{aligned}\n\n\n🎯 Complete Inverse: $\\mathbf{A^{-1} = \\frac{1}{4} \\begin{bmatrix} 3 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}}$. Verified: $|A| = 4 ≠ 0$."
