        "title": "3. Consistency of System of Linear Equations AX = B",
        "content": "### 1. Comprehensive Theory: System of Linear Equations $AX = B$\n\nA system of $m$ linear equations in $n$ unknowns $x₁, x₂, \..., xₙ$ is represented in matrix notation as:\n$$\\begin{bmatrix} a_{11} & a_{12} & \... & a_{1n} \\\\ a_{21} & a_{22} & \... & a_{2n} \\\\ \⋮ & \⋮ & \⋱ & \⋮ \\\\ a_{m1} & a_{m2} & \... & a_{mn} \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} x₁ \\\\ x₂ \\\\ \⋮ \\\\ xₙ \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} b_1 \\\\ b_2 \\\\ \⋮ \\\\ b_m \\end{bmatrix} \\iff \\mathbf{A X = B}$$\n\n#### The Rouché–Capelli Theorem:\n> 🟢 **Theorem Statement:** A system of linear equations $A X = B$ is **consistent** (possesses at least one solution) if and only if the rank of the coefficient matrix $A$ equals the rank of the augmented matrix $[A \| B]$:\n> $$\\mathbf{ρ(A) = ρ([A \| B])}$$\n\n---\n\n### 2. Complete Classification of Solution Regimes\n\n```\n                                  [ System AX = B ]\n                                          |\n                 +------------------------+------------------------+\n                 |                                                 |\n         rho(A) != rho([A|B])                              rho(A) == rho([A|B])\n                 |                                                 |\n         [ INCONSISTENT ]                                   [ CONSISTENT ]\n         Zero Solutions!                                           |\n         (Hyperplanes parallel                             +-------+-------+\n          or non-intersecting)                             |               |\n                                                      rho(A) == n     rho(A) = r < n\n                                                           |               |\n                                                   [ UNIQUE SOLN ]  [ INFINITE SOLNS ]\n                                                   Single point     (n - r) free parameters\n                                                   intersection     Forms an affine subspace\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Homogeneous Linear Systems $AX = 0$\n\nFor $AX = 0$, $B = 0$. Since augmenting a column of zeros cannot increase matrix rank, $ρ(A) = ρ([A \| 0])$ always holds. Therefore:\n\A homogeneous system is ALWAYS consistent. It always has at least the solution  X = 0.\n\n#### Nature of Solutions for Square Systems (n × n):\n1. **Trivial Solution (X = 0):**\n   - Occurs when ρ(A) = n ⇔ |A| ≠ 0 (Non-singular matrix).\n   - The columns of A are linearly independent; the only linear combination giving zero is x₁ = x₂ = ... = xₙ = 0.\n2. **Non-Trivial Solutions (Infinite Non-Zero Solutions):**\n   - Occurs when ρ(A)  🔴 **Examiner's Trap:** When an exam problem asks: *\"Find values of $k$ for which the equations have non-zero solutions\"*, students waste time performing row echelon operations. Simply set the determinant to zero: $|A| = 0$!\n\n---\n\n---\n\n\n**Problem:**\nDetermine whether the system has a non-trivial solution:\n$$\\begin{cases} 2x + 3y = 0 \\\\ 4x + 6y = 0 \\end{cases}$$\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Evaluate Coefficient Determinant $|A|$**\n$$|A| = \\begin{vmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6 \\end{vmatrix} = (2)(6) - (3)(4) = 12 - 12 = 0$$\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Apply Homogeneous Consistency Rule**\nSince $|A| = 0$, $ρ(A) = 1 < n = 2$.\nHence, the system has **infinitely many non-trivial solutions**.\nExpress in parametric form: $2x + 3y = 0 ⇒ x = -\\frac{3}{2}k, \; y = k$ ($k \∈ \\mathbb{R}$).\n\n\n🎯 Non-trivial solution exists: $\\mathbf{(x, y) = (-3k/2, \, k)}$ for any scalar $k ≠ 0$.\n\n\n\n\n**University Question (8 Marks):**\nTest the consistency of the following system and solve using Gauss Elimination:\n$$\\begin{cases} x + y + z = 6 \\\\ 2x - y + z = 3 \\\\ x - 2y + 3z = 6 \\end{cases}$$\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Construct Augmented Matrix [A \| B]**\n$$[A \mid B] = \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 & 6 \\end{array} \\right]$$\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Apply Row Operations (R₂ → R₂ - 2R₁, R₃ → R₃ - R₁)**\n$$\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\end{array} \\right] \\xrightarrow{R_3 \\to R_3 - R_2} \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 9 \\end{array} \\right]$$\n\n**Step 3 🎯: Check Ranks & Back Substitution**\n- ρ(A) = 3, ρ([A \| B]) = 3 = n (number of unknowns).\n- Consistent with a **Unique Solution**!\n  - Row 3: 3z = 9 ⇒ \z = 3\n  - Row 2: -3y - 3 = -9 ⇒ -3y = -6 ⇒ \y = 2\n  - Row 1: x + 2 + 3 = 6 ⇒ x = 1\n\n\n🎯 Consistent & Unique: $\\mathbf{x = 1, \; y = 2, \; z = 3}$.\n\n\n\n\n**University Question (10 Marks - Classic MMU Exam Problem):**\nInvestigate for what values of $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ the system of equations:\n$$\\begin{cases} x + y + z = 6 \\\\ x + 2y + 3z = 10 \\\\ x + 2y + \\lambda z = \\mu \\end{cases}$$\nhas: (i) No solution, (ii) A unique solution, (iii) Infinitely many solutions.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Setup Augmented Matrix and Echelon Reduction**\n$$[A \\mid B] = \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 2 & \\lambda & \\mu \\end{array} \\right] \\xrightarrow{\\substack{R_2 \\to R_2 - R_1 \\\\ R_3 \\to R_3 - R_1}} \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\lambda - 1 & \\mu - 6 \\end{array} \\right]$$\n\n$$\\xrightarrow{R_3 \\to R_3 - R_2} \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\lambda - 3 & \\mu - 10 \\end{array} \\right]$$\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Case Analysis by Rouché–Capelli Theorem**\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{1. Case (i) No Solution (Inconsistent): } & \\lambda = 3, \\; \\mu \\ne 10 \\implies \\rho(A) = 2 \\ne \\rho([A \\mid B]) = 3 \\\\\n\\text{2. Case (ii) Unique Solution: } & \\lambda \\ne 3, \\; \\forall \\mu \\implies \\rho(A) = \\rho([A \\mid B]) = 3 = n \\\\\n\\text{3. Case (iii) Infinite Solutions: } & \\lambda = 3, \\; \\mu = 10 \\implies \\rho(A) = \\rho([A \\mid B]) = 2 < n \\; (z = k)\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\n<div class=\"example-answer-box\">\n🎯 **Verified Result:** Question 17 step-by-step resolution per MMDU university marking scheme.\n</div>\n</example>\n\n---\n\n<example level=\"hard\" title=\"Question 21 (6 Marks): Verification of Cayley-Hamilton Theorem & Inverse / Higher Power\">\n**Question:**  \nFor matrix A = [[1, 2, 1], [0, 1, -1], [3, -1, 1]]. (1) Verify Cayley-Hamilton Theorem, (2) Evaluate A⁻¹, (3) Compute A⁴.\n\n> 📊 **Question Schematic / State Specification:**\n```\nQuestion Characteristic Equation: |A - λ I| = -λ³ + S₁ λ² - S₂ λ + |A| = 0.\n```\n\n---\n\n**Step-by-Step Solution:**\n\n> ⚡ **Solution Analytical Derivation & Schematic:**\n```\nSolution Cayley-Hamilton Full Matrix Derivation:\n\n        `1. Invariants: S₁ = Tr(A) = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3`\n\n        `S₂ = M₁₁ + M₂₂ + M₃₃ = (1 - 1) + (1 - 3) + (1 - 0) = 0 - 2 + 1 = -1`\n\n        `|A| = 1(0) - 2(3) + 1(-3) = -9`\n\n        `Char Eq: λ³ - 3λ² - λ + 9 = 0 ⇒ A³ - 3A² - A + 9I = 0`\n\n        `2. Evaluate A² = \\begin{bmatrix} 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 5 \\end{bmatrix}, \ A³ = \\begin{bmatrix} 4 & 11 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -2 & -7 \\\\ 21 & 11 & 7 \\end{bmatrix}`\n\n        `Check: A³ - 3A² - A + 9I = 0 ⇒ \VERIFIED!`\n\n        `3. Inverse: A⁻¹ = -\\frac{1}{9}[A² - 3A - I] = \\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -7 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}`\n\n        `4. Higher Power: A⁴ = 3A³ + A² - 9A`\n```\n\nCayley-Hamilton is verified with zero remainder matrix, yielding the exact inverse and fourth power matrices.\n\n<div class=\"example-answer-box\">\n🎯 **Verified Result:** Question 21 step-by-step resolution per MMDU university marking scheme.\n</div>\n</example>\n\n---"
