        "sectionId": "m1-u3-s2",
        "title": "2. Euler's Theorem on Homogeneous Functions",
        "content": "### 1. Homogeneous Functions & Euler's Theorem: Rigorous Analysis\n\n#### Definition of a Homogeneous Function:\n> 🟢 **Definition:** A multivariable function $f(x, y)$ is said to be **homogeneous of degree $n$** if for any scalar $t > 0$:\n> $$\\mathbf{f(t x, t y) = t^n f(x, y)}$$\n> Equivalently, $f(x, y)$ can always be factored into the canonical form:\n> $$\\mathbf{f(x, y) = x^n \, \\phi\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) = y^n \, \\psi\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)}$$\n\n---\n\n### 2. Euler's Theorem on Homogeneous Functions (First Order)\n\n> 🟢 **Theorem Statement:** If $u = f(x, y)$ is a homogeneous function of $x$ and $y$ of degree $n$ possessing continuous partial derivatives, then:\n> $$\\mathbf{x \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ x} + y \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ y} = n \, u}$$\n\n#### Complete Analytical Proof:\nSince $u(x, y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$, we can write:\n$$u(x, y) = x^n \\phi(v) \   \\text{where } v = \\frac{y}{x}$$\nDifferentiating partially with respect to $x$:\n$$\\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ x} = n x^{n-1} \\phi(v) + x^n \\phi'(v) \\frac{\∂ v}{\∂ x} = n x^{n-1} \\phi(v) + x^n \\phi'(v) \\left(-\\frac{y}{x²}\\right) = n x^{n-1} \\phi(v) - x^{n-1} y \\phi'(v)$$\nMultiply by $x$:\n$$x \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ x} = n x^n \\phi(v) - x^n y \\phi'(v) \   \\text{--- (Eq 1)}$$\nDifferentiating partially with respect to $y$:\n$$\\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ y} = x^n \\phi'(v) \\frac{\∂ v}{\∂ y} = x^n \\phi'(v) \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) = x^{n-1} \\phi'(v)$$\nMultiply by $y$:\n$$y \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ y} = x^n y \\phi'(v) \   \\text{--- (Eq 2)}$$\nAdding (Eq 1) and (Eq 2):\n$$x \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ x} + y \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ y} = n x^n \\phi(v) = \\mathbf{n \, u} \   \\blacksquare$$\n\n---\n\n### 3. Second-Order Extension of Euler's Theorem\n\nDifferentiating the first-order Euler relation partially with respect to $x$ and $y$ and combining yields the **Second-Order Euler Formula**:\n$$\\mathbf{x² \\frac{\∂^2 u}{\∂ x²} + 2xy \\frac{\∂^2 u}{\∂ x \∂ y} + y² \\frac{\∂^2 u}{\∂ y²} = n(n - 1) u}$$\n\n#### 3-Variable Generalization:\nFor a homogeneous function $u(x, y, z)$ of degree $n$:\n$$\\mathbf{x \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ x} + y \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ y} + z \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ z} = n \, u}$$\n\n> 🔴 **Examiner's Composite Function Trap:** If $u = \\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{x² + y²}{x + y}\\right)$, $u$ is NOT homogeneous! But $z = \\sin u = \\frac{x² + y²}{x + y}$ is homogeneous of degree $n = 2 - 1 = 1$.\n> Applying Euler's theorem to $z$:\n> $$x \\frac{\∂ z}{\∂ x} + y \\frac{\∂ z}{\∂ y} = 1 \· z ⇒ x (\\cos u) \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ x} + y (\\cos u) \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ y} = \\sin u ⇒ \\mathbf{x \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ x} + y \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ y} = \\tan u}$$\n\n---\n\n---\n\n\n**Problem:**\nVerify Euler's Theorem for $u = x³ + 3x^2 y + y^3$.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Degree of Homogeneity**\n$$u(tx, ty) = (tx)^3 + 3(tx)^2(ty) + (ty)^3 = t^3 [x³ + 3x^2y + y^3] = t^3 u$$\nHence, $u$ is homogeneous of degree $\\mathbf{n = 3}$.\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Evaluate First Partial Derivatives**\n$$u_x = 3x^2 + 6xy, \     u_y = 3x^2 + 3y^2$$\n\n**Step 3 🎯: Compute $x u_x + y u_y$**\n$$x(3x^2 + 6xy) + y(3x^2 + 3y^2) = 3x^3 + 6x^2y + 3x^2y + 3y^3 = 3(x³ + 3x^2y + y^3) = \\mathbf{3u}$$\nMatches $n \· u = 3u$!\n\n\n🎯 Euler's Theorem Verified: $\\mathbf{x \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ x} + y \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ y} = 3u}$.\n\n\n\n\n**University Question (8 Marks - MMU Question Paper):**\nIf u = \\sin⁻¹\\left( (x + 2y + 3z / √(x⁸ + y⁸ + z⁸)) \\right), prove that x uₓ + y uy + z uz = -3 \\tan u.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Convert to Homogeneous Function v = \\sin u**\nv = \\sin u = (x + 2y + 3z / √(x⁸ + y⁸ + z⁸))\nDegree of numerator is 1; degree of denominator is √(t⁸) = t⁴.\nDegree of v: n = 1 - 4 = -3.\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Apply Euler's Theorem to v**\nx (∂ v / ∂ x) + y (∂ v / ∂ y) + z (∂ v / ∂ z) = -3 v\nSince v = \\sin u, by chain rule (∂ v / ∂ x) = \\cos u (∂ u / ∂ x):\nx \\cos u uₓ + y \\cos u uy + z \\cos u uz = -3 \\sin u\n\n**Step 3 🎯: Divide across by \\cos u**\nx uₓ + y uy + z uz = -3 (\\sin u{\\cos u} = -3 \\tan u / )$\n\n<div class=\"example-answer-box\">\n🎯 Result Proved: $\\mathbf{x \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ x} + y \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ y} + z \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ z} = -3\\tan u}$.\n</div>\n</example>\n\n<example level=\"hard\" title=\"Level 3 (Hard): Euler's Second-Order Deduction Formula\">\n**University Question (10 Marks - MMU Previous Paper):**\nIf u = \\tan⁻¹\\left( \\frac{x³ + y³}{x - y} \\right), prove that:\nx² (∂^2 u / ∂ x²) + 2xy (∂^2 u / ∂ x ∂ y) + y² (∂^2 u / ∂ y²) = \\sin 2u (2\\cos² u - 1) = \\sin 4u  or  2\\sin u \\cos 3u\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Define z = \\tan u**\nz = \\frac{x³ + y³}{x - y} ⇒ z  is homogeneous of degree  n = 3 - 1 = 2\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: First Order Euler's Deduction**\nx zₓ + y zy = 2z ⇒ x \\sec² u uₓ + y \\sec² u uy = 2\\tan u\nx uₓ + y uy = (2\\tan u / \\sec² u) = 2\\sin u \\cos u = \\\sin 2u = f(u)\n\n**Step 3 🎯: Second Order Euler's Operator Identity**\nx² uₓₓ + 2xy uₓy + y² uyy = f(u)[f'(u) - 1]\nHere f(u) = \\sin 2u ⇒ f'(u) = 2\\cos 2u.\n= \\sin 2u [2\\cos 2u - 1]\nUsing identity \\sin A \\cos B = ...:\n= 2\\sin 2u \\cos 2u - \\sin 2u = \\\sin 4u - \\sin 2u\n\n<div class=\"example-answer-box\">\n🎯 Second-Order Euler Identity Proved: $\\mathbf{x² u_{xx} + 2xy u_{xy} + y² u_{yy} = \\sin 2u (2\\cos 2u - 1)}$.\n</div>"
