        "sectionId": "m1-u3-s3",
        "title": "3. Total Derivative & Lagrange Multipliers",
        "content": "### 1. Total Differential, Chain Rule & Extrema of Multivariable Functions\n\n#### A. The Total Differential ($du$):\nLet $u = f(x, y)$ be a function of two independent variables $x$ and $y$. The **Total Differential** $du$ represents the principal linear part of the increment $\\Delta u$:\n$$\\mathbf{du = \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ x} dx + \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ y} dy}$$\nFor a function of three variables $u = f(x, y, z)$:\n$$\\mathbf{du = \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ x} dx + \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ y} dy + \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ z} dz}$$\n\n#### B. Chain Rule for Composite Functions:\n1. **Case 1 (Single Intermediate Parameter):** If $u = f(x, y)$ where $x = x(t)$ and $y = y(t)$:\n   $$\\mathbf{\\frac{du}{dt} = \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ x}\\frac{dx}{dt} + \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ y}\\frac{dy}{dt}}$$\n2. **Case 2 (Two Intermediate Parameters):** If $u = f(x, y)$ where $x = x(r, θ)$ and $y = y(r, θ)$:\n   $$\\mathbf{\\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ r} = \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ x}\\frac{\∂ x}{\∂ r} + \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ y}\\frac{\∂ y}{\∂ r}}, \     \\mathbf{\\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ θ} = \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ x}\\frac{\∂ x}{\∂ θ} + \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ y}\\frac{\∂ y}{\∂ θ}}$$\n\n---\n\n### 2. Unconstrained Extrema & The Second Derivative Hessian Test\n\nTo locate the local maxima, local minima, and saddle points of $z = f(x, y)$:\n1. **Find Stationary (Critical) Points:** Solve simultaneously:\n   $$\\mathbf{f_x = \\frac{\∂ f}{\∂ x} = 0} \   \\text{and} \   \\mathbf{f_y = \\frac{\∂ f}{\∂ y} = 0}$$\n2. **Compute Second-Order Partials at Critical Point $(a, b)$:**\n   $$\\mathbf{r = f_{xx} = \\frac{\∂^2 f}{\∂ x²}}, \   \\mathbf{s = f_{xy} = \\frac{\∂^2 f}{\∂ x \∂ y}}, \   \\mathbf{t = f_{yy} = \\frac{\∂^2 f}{\∂ y²}}$$\n3. **Evaluate the Discriminant (Hessian Determinant) $\\Delta = rt - s^2$:**\n\n| Condition on $\\Delta = rt - s^2$ | Condition on $r$ | Nature of Critical Point $(a, b)$ |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **$\\mathbf{rt - s^2 > 0}$** | $\\mathbf{r < 0}$ ($f_{xx} < 0$) | Local Maximum (Peak of a hill) |\n| **$\\mathbf{rt - s^2 > 0}$** | $\\mathbf{r > 0}$ ($f_{xx} > 0$) | Local Minimum (Bottom of a bowl) |\n| **$\\mathbf{rt - s^2 < 0}$** | Any sign | Saddle Point (Pringles chip / horse saddle) |\n| **$\\mathbf{rt - s^2 = 0}$** | Indeterminate | **Test Fails** (Higher order investigation required) |\n\n---\n\n### 3. Constrained Optimization: Lagrange's Method of Undetermined Multipliers\n\nTo maximize or minimize an objective function $f(x, y, z)$ subject to the constraint equation $\\phi(x, y, z) = 0$:\n\n#### Method Pipeline:\n1. Construct the **Auxiliary Lagrange Function**:\n   $$\\mathbf{F(x, y, z, λ) = f(x, y, z) + λ \· \\phi(x, y, z)}$$\n   where $λ$ is called the **Lagrange Multiplier**.\n2. Set all first partial derivatives to zero:\n   $$\\mathbf{\\frac{\∂ F}{\∂ x} = f_x + λ \\phi_x = 0}, \   \\mathbf{\\frac{\∂ F}{\∂ y} = f_y + λ \\phi_y = 0}, \   \\mathbf{\\frac{\∂ F}{\∂ z} = f_z + λ \\phi_z = 0}, \   \\mathbf{\\frac{\∂ F}{\∂ λ} = \\phi(x, y, z) = 0}$$\n3. Eliminate $λ$ between equations to solve for optimal coordinates $(x^*, y^*, z^*)$ and evaluate optimum $f(x^*, y^*, z^*)$.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n\n**Problem:**\nFind $\\frac{du}{dt}$ if $u = x² + y²$ where $x = at^2$ and $y = 2at$.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Total Derivative Formula**\n$$\\frac{du}{dt} = \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ x} \\frac{dx}{dt} + \\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ y} \\frac{dy}{dt}$$\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Calculate Derivatives**\n- $\\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ x} = 2x = 2at^2$, $\   \\frac{dx}{dt} = 2at$\n- $\\frac{\∂ u}{\∂ y} = 2y = 4at$, $\   \\frac{dy}{dt} = 2a$\n\n**Step 3 🎯: Combine**\n$$\\frac{du}{dt} = (2at^2)(2at) + (4at)(2a) = 4a^2 t^3 + 8a^2 t = \\mathbf{4a^2 t(t² + 2)}$$\n\n\n🎯 Total Derivative: $\\mathbf{\\frac{du}{dt} = 4a^2 t(t² + 2)}$.\n\n\n\n\n**University Question (8 Marks):**\nFind the local maximum and minimum values of f(x, y) = x³ + y³ - 3xy.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Find Critical (Stationary) Points**\nfₓ = 3x² - 3y = 0 ⇒ y = x²\nfy = 3y² - 3x = 0 ⇒ 3(x²)² - 3x = 0 ⇒ 3x(x³ - 1) = 0\nCritical points: (0, 0) and (1, 1).\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Compute Second Partials r, s, t**\n- r = fₓₓ = 6x\n- s = fₓy = -3\n- t = fyy = 6y\n- Discriminant: Δ = rt - s² = (6x)(6y) - (-3)² = 36xy - 9\n\n**Step 3 🎯: Test Points**\n1. **At (0, 0):** Δ = 0 - 9 = -9  0, and r = 6 > 0 ⇒ (1, 1)  is a Local Minimum.\n   f\ₘᵢₙ = 1³ + 1³ - 3(1)(1) = 1 + 1 - 3 = -1\n\n\n🎯 Saddle point: $(0, 0)$ | Local Minimum at $(1, 1)$ with $f_{\\min} = -1$.\n\n\n\n\n**University Question (10 Marks - MMU Question Paper):**\nFind the maximum volume of a rectangular box open at the top, having a given surface area S = 108\,\cm², using Lagrange's method of multipliers.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Objective and Constraint Functions**\n- Volume to maximize: V(x, y, z) = x y z\n- Surface area of open box: φ(x, y, z) = xy + 2yz + 2zx - 108 = 0\n- Auxiliary Lagrange Function:\n  F(x, y, z, λ) = xyz + λ (xy + 2yz + 2zx - 108)\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Set First Partials to Zero**\n1. Fₓ = yz + λ(y + 2z) = 0 ⇒ λ = -\\frac{yz}{y + 2z}\n2. Fy = xz + λ(x + 2z) = 0 ⇒ λ = -\\frac{xz}{x + 2z}\n3. Fz = xy + λ(2y + 2x) = 0 ⇒ λ = -(xy / 2(x + y))\n\n**Step 3 🎯: Equate Expressions**\nFrom (1) and (2): \\frac{yz}{y + 2z} = \\frac{xz}{x + 2z} ⇒ y(x + 2z) = x(y + 2z) ⇒ x = y.\nFrom (2) and (3) with x = y:\n\\frac{xz}{x + 2z} = \\frac{x²}{4x} = \\frac{x}{4} ⇒ 4z = x + 2z ⇒ x = 2z ⇒ z = x/2\n\n**Step 4 🏁: Substitute into Constraint S = 108**\nx(x) + 2(x)\\frac{x}{2} + 2\\frac{x}{2}(x) = x² + x² + x² = 3x² = 108\nx² = 36 ⇒ x = 6, \   \y = 6, \   \z = 3\nV\ₘₐₓ ₌ ₍₆₎₍₆₎₍₃₎ ₌ ₁₀₈\,\cₘ³\n\n\n🎯 Dimensions: $x = 6\,\\text{cm}, y = 6\,\\text{cm}, z = 3\,\\text{cm}$ | Maximum Volume: $\\mathbf{V_{\\max} = 108\,\\text{cm}^3}$."
