        "sectionId": "m1-u4-s3",
        "title": "3. Fourier Series: Euler's Formulae & Discontinuity",
        "content": "### 1. Fourier Series: Foundations & Euler's Formulas\n\nA **Fourier Series** decomposes any periodic function $f(x)$ of period $T = 2π$ (or $2l$) into an infinite sum of orthogonal harmonic sines and cosines.\n\n#### The Fourier Series Representation in $(-π, π)$ or $(0, 2π)$:\n$$\\mathbf{f(x) = \\frac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\\infty \\left[ a_n \\cos(nx) + b_n \\sin(nx) \\right]}$$\n\n#### Euler's Integral Formulas for Coefficients:\nUsing the orthogonality of trigonometric basis functions ($\\int_{-π}^π \\cos(mx)\\cos(nx)dx = π \\delta_{mn}$, etc.):\n$$\\mathbf{a_0 = \\frac{1}{π} \\int_{-π}^π f(x) \, dx = \\frac{1}{π} \\int_0^{2π} f(x) \, dx}$$\n$$\\mathbf{a_n = \\frac{1}{π} \\int_{-π}^π f(x) \\cos(nx) \, dx = \\frac{1}{π} \\int_0^{2π} f(x) \\cos(nx) \, dx}$$\n$$\\mathbf{b_n = \\frac{1}{π} \\int_{-π}^π f(x) \\sin(nx) \, dx = \\frac{1}{π} \\int_0^{2π} f(x) \\sin(nx) \, dx}$$\n\n---\n\n### 2. Dirichlet's Conditions for Fourier Convergence\n\n> 🟢 **Theorem:** Any function $f(x)$ defined on $(-π, π)$ can be expanded in a convergent Fourier series if it satisfies **Dirichlet's Conditions**:\n> 1. $f(x)$ is **single-valued** and periodic with period $2π$.\n> 2. $f(x)$ is **piecewise continuous** (has at most a finite number of finite jump discontinuities in any one period).\n> 3. $f(x)$ has at most a **finite number of local maxima and minima** in any one period.\n\n#### Convergence at Points of Discontinuity:\n- **At a point of continuity $x_0$:** The Fourier series converges to the exact value: $S(x_0) = f(x_0)$.\n- **At a finite jump discontinuity $x = c$:** The Fourier series converges to the **arithmetic average of the left-hand and right-hand limits**:\n  $$\\mathbf{S(c) = \\frac{f(c^+) + f(c^-)}{2} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left[ \\lim_{h \→ 0^+} f(c + h) + \\lim_{h \→ 0^+} f(c - h) \\right]}$$\n- **At the interval boundaries ($x = -π$ and $x = π$):**\n  $$\\mathbf{S(π) = S(-π) = \\frac{f(-π^+) + f(π^-)}{2}}$$\n\n---\n\n---\n\n\n**Problem:**\nFind the Fourier series of $f(x) = x$ in the interval $(-π, π)$.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Recognize Symmetry**\n$f(-x) = -x = -f(x)$, so $f(x)$ is an **ODD function**!\n- $a_0 = 0$\n- $a_n = 0$\nWe only need to evaluate $b_n$!\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Compute $b_n$ by Integration by Parts**\n$$b_n = \\frac{2}{π} \\int_0^π x \\sin(nx) \, dx = \\frac{2}{π} \\left[ x \\left( -\\frac{\\cos nx}{n} \\right) - (1) \\left( -\\frac{\\sin nx}{n^2} \\right) \\right]_0^π$$\n$$= \\frac{2}{π} \\left[ -\\frac{π \\cos(nπ)}{n} - 0 \\right] = -\\frac{2 (-1)^n}{n} = \\mathbf{\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n}}$$\n\n**Step 3 🎯: Fourier Expansion**\n$$\\mathbf{f(x) = 2 \\left( \\sin x - \\frac{\\sin 2x}{2} + \\frac{\\sin 3x}{3} - \\frac{\\sin 4x}{4} + \... \\right)}$$\n\n\n🎯 Fourier Series: $\\mathbf{x = 2 \\sum_{n=1}^\\\infty \\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n} \\sin(nx)}$.\n\n\n\n\n**University Question (8 Marks):**\nFind the Fourier series for f(x) = \\begin{cases} -k, & -π \n🎯 Fourier Series: $\\mathbf{f(x) = \\frac{4k}{π} \\sum_{n=1,3,5\...} \\frac{\\sin nx}{n}}$ | At discontinuity $x = 0$: $f(0) = 0$.\n\n\n\n\n**University Question (10 Marks - MMU Question Paper):**\nObtain the Fourier series for f(x) = x² in (-π, π). Hence deduce that:\n\\frac{1}{1²} + \\frac{1}{2²} + \\frac{1}{3²} + \\frac{1}{4²} + ... = \\frac{π²}{6}\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Symmetry & Coefficients**\nf(-x) = (-x)² = x² = f(x), so f(x) is an **EVEN function** ⇒ \bₙ = 0.\na₀ = \\frac{2}{π} \int_0^{\pi} x² dx = \\frac{2}{π} \\left[ \\frac{x³}{3} \\right]_0^π = \(2π²(3) / )\naₙ = \\frac{2}{π} \int_0^{\pi} x² \\cos(nx) \, dx\nUsing generalized integration by parts (u v₁ - u' v₂ + u'' v₃):\n= \\frac{2}{π} \\left[ x² \\left((\\sin nx / n)\\right) - (2x)\\left(-(\\cos nx / n²)\\right) + (2)\\left(-(\\sin nx / n³)\\right) \\right]_0^π\n= \\frac{2}{π} \\left[ (2π \\cos(nπ) / n²) \\right] = \(4(-1)ⁿ{n²} / )\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Fourier Expansion**\nf(x) = (a₀ / 2) + ∑_(n=1)^\infty aₙ \\cos(nx) = \\frac{π²}{3} + 4 ∑_(n=1)^\infty ((-1)ⁿ / n²) \\cos(nx)\nx² = \\frac{π²}{3} - 4 \\left( (\\cos x / 1²) - (\\cos 2x / 2²) + (\\cos 3x / 3²) - ... \\right)\n\n**Step 3 🎯: Deduce ∑ \\frac{1}{n²} = \\frac{π²}{6} by setting x = π**\nAt x = π: f(π) = π². Since \\cos(nπ) = (-1)ⁿ:\nπ² = \\frac{π²}{3} - 4 \\left( \\frac{-1}{1²} - \\frac{1}{2²} - \\frac{1}{3²} - ... \\right) = \\frac{π²}{3} + 4 ∑_(n=1)^\infty \\frac{1}{n²}\nπ² - \\frac{π²}{3} = 4 ∑_(n=1)^\infty \\frac{1}{n²} ⇒ \\frac{2π²}{3} = 4 ∑_(n=1)^\infty \\frac{1}{n²} ⇒ \∑_{n=1^\infty \\frac{1}{n²} = \\frac{π²}{6}}\n\n\n🎯 Fourier Series: $x² = \\frac{π^2}{3} + 4 \\sum_{n=1}^\\\infty \\frac{(-1)^n}{n^2} \\cos(nx)$ | Deduced: $\\mathbf{\\sum_{n=1}^\\\infty \\frac{1}{n^2} = \\frac{π^2}{6}}$."
