        "content": "### MMDU Official University Solved Examination Questions (Unit 4)\n**Course:** Mathematics I (`BMAT-001`)  \n**Pattern:** Sections A (1 Mark Compulsory), B (2 Marks Analytical), C (6 Marks In-Depth), D (6 Marks Design/Long)\n\n---\n<example level=\"easy\" title=\"Question 10 (1 Mark): Failure of Ratio Test on p-Series\">\n**Question:**  \nWhy does D'Alembert's Ratio Test fail to determine the convergence of the p-series &Sigma; \\frac{1}{n^p}?\n\n> 📊 **Question Schematic / State Specification:**\n```\nQuestion Limit Ratio: uₙ / uₙ₊₁ = \\frac{1 + 1}{n}ᵖ as n ➔ \\infty.\n```\n\n---\n\n**Step-by-Step Solution:**\n\n> ⚡ **Solution Analytical Derivation & Schematic:**\n```\nSolution Indeterminate Limit Ratio:\n\n        `L = \\lim_{n \to \infty} (uₙ / uₙ₊₁) = \\lim_{n \to \infty} \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{n}\\right)ᵖ = 1ᵖ = 1 ⇒ Test Fails for ALL  p!`\n```\n\nBecause the limit ratio evaluates identically to 1 for every real exponent p, the ratio test provides no diagnostic information (Raabe's or integral test must be applied).\n\n<div class=\"example-answer-box\">\n🎯 **Verified Result:** Question 10 step-by-step resolution per MMDU university marking scheme.\n</div>\n</example>\n\n---\n\n<example level=\"easy\" title=\"Question 11 (1 Mark): Fourier Coefficients for an Odd Function\">\n**Question:**  \nState the values of Fourier coefficients a₀ and aₙ for an odd function f(x) in [-π, π].\n\n> 📊 **Question Schematic / State Specification:**\n```\nQuestion Parity Symmetry: f(-x) = -f(x) across origin in symmetrical interval [-π, π].\n```\n\n---\n\n**Step-by-Step Solution:**\n\n> ⚡ **Solution Analytical Derivation & Schematic:**\n```\nSolution Symmetric Integral Cancellation:\n\n        `a₀ = \\frac{1}{π}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi} f(x) dx = 0  (Odd integrand)`\n\n        `aₙ = \\frac{1}{π}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi} f(x)\\cos(nx) dx = 0  (Odd  ×  Even = Odd integrand)`\n```\n\nFor any odd function, `a₀ = 0` and `aₙ = 0` identically. The Fourier expansion contains purely sine terms (Fourier Sine Series).\n\n<div class=\"example-answer-box\">\n🎯 **Verified Result:** Question 11 step-by-step resolution per MMDU university marking scheme.\n</div>\n</example>\n\n---\n\n<example level=\"easy\" title=\"Question 12 (1 Mark): Alternating Harmonic Series Convergence\">\n**Question:**  \nClassify the series &Sigma; (-1)ⁿ⁻¹ / n as conditionally or absolutely convergent.\n\n> 📊 **Question Schematic / State Specification:**\n```\nQuestion Convergence Split: &Sigma; (-1)ⁿ⁻¹/n converges to ln(2) | &Sigma; |(-1)ⁿ⁻¹/n| = &Sigma; \\frac{1}{n} diverges.\n```\n\n---\n\n**Step-by-Step Solution:**\n\n> ⚡ **Solution Analytical Derivation & Schematic:**\n```\nSolution Dual Test Classification:\n\n        `1. Leibnitz Test: uₙ = 1/n ⇒ uₙ₊₁ ≤ uₙ  and  \\lim \\frac{1}{n} = 0 ⇒ Convergent!`\n\n        `2. Absolute Series: ∑ |uₙ| = ∑ \\frac{1}{n} ⇒ \Divergent harmonic series (p=1)`\n```\n\nBecause the alternating series converges but its absolute series diverges, it is strictly **Conditionally Convergent**.\n\n<div class=\"example-answer-box\">\n🎯 **Verified Result:** Question 12 step-by-step resolution per MMDU university marking scheme.\n</div>\n</example>\n\n---\n\n<example level=\"medium\" title=\"Question 16 (2 Marks): Cauchy's Root Test on Infinite Series\">\n**Question:**  \nTest the convergence of the series &Sigma; [ (n / (n + 1))ⁿ × \\frac{1}{2ⁿ} ] using Cauchy's Root Test.\n\n> 📊 **Question Schematic / State Specification:**\n```\nQuestion nth Power Term: General term uₙ has overall exponent n.\n```\n\n---\n\n**Step-by-Step Solution:**\n\n> ⚡ **Solution Analytical Derivation & Schematic:**\n```\nSolution nth Root Limit Trace:\n\n        `(uₙ)¹/ⁿ = \\left[ \\left(\\frac{n}{n+1}\\right)ⁿ · \\frac{1}{2ⁿ} \\right]¹/ⁿ = \\frac{n}{n+1} · \\frac{1}{2} = (1 / 1 + 1/n) · \\frac{1}{2}`\n\n        `L = \\lim_{n \to \infty} (uₙ)¹/ⁿ = \\frac{1}{1 + 0} · \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{2} &lt; 1`\n```\n\nBecause limit ratio L =\\frac{1}{2}&lt; 1, the series is strictly **Convergent**.\n\n<div class=\"example-answer-box\">\n🎯 **Verified Result:** Question 16 step-by-step resolution per MMDU university marking scheme.\n</div>\n</example>\n\n---\n\n<example level=\"hard\" title=\"Question 20 (4 Marks): Raabe's Test on Higher-Order Series\">\n**Question:**  \nTest the convergence of the series: 1 + \\frac{1}{2} · \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1 · 3}{2 · 4} · \\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{1 · 3 · 5}{2 · 4 · 6} · \\frac{1}{7} + ...\n\n> 📊 **Question Schematic / State Specification:**\n```\nQuestion General Term Expression: uₙ = \\frac{1 · 3 · 5 ... (2n-1)}{2 · 4 · 6 ... (2n)} × [ 1 / (2n+1) ].\n```\n\n---\n\n**Step-by-Step Solution:**\n\n> ⚡ **Solution Analytical Derivation & Schematic:**\n```\nSolution Ratio Failure & Raabe's Resolution:\n\n        `(uₙ / uₙ₊₁) = \\frac{2n+2}{2n+1} · \\frac{2n+3}{2n+1} = \\frac{4n² + 10n + 6}{4n² + 4n + 1} ⇒ \\lim_{n \to \infty} (uₙ / uₙ₊₁) = 1  (Ratio Test FAILS!)`\n\n        `Apply Raabe's Test:  L = \\lim_{n \to \infty} n \\left[ (uₙ / uₙ₊₁) - 1 \\right] = \\lim_{n \to \infty} n \\left[ \\frac{6n + 5}{4n² + 4n + 1} \\right] = \\lim_{n \to \infty} \\frac{6n² + 5n}{4n² + 4n + 1} = \\frac{6}{4} = \\frac{3}{2}`\n\n        `Since L =\\frac{3}{2}= 1.5 &gt; 1 ⇒ CONVERGENT by Raabe's Test!`\n```\n\nBecause the Raabe limit `L = 1.5 &gt; 1`, the infinite series converges.\n\n<div class=\"example-answer-box\">\n🎯 **Verified Result:** Question 20 step-by-step resolution per MMDU university marking scheme.\n</div>\n</example>\n\n---\n\n<example level=\"hard\" title=\"Question 24 (6 Marks): Full Fourier Expansion of Discontinuous Square Wave\">\n**Question:**  \nObtain the Fourier series expansion for the square wave function: f(x) = -k for -π &lt; x &lt; 0, and f(x) = +k for 0 &lt; x &lt; π. Hence deduce: 1 - 1/3 + 1/5 - 1/7 + ... = π / 4.\n\n> 📊 **Question Schematic / State Specification:**\n```\nQuestion Square Wave Symmetry: f(x) is an odd function ⟹ a₀ = 0 and aₙ = 0.\n```\n\n---\n\n**Step-by-Step Solution:**\n\n> ⚡ **Solution Analytical Derivation & Schematic:**\n```\nSolution Fourier Sine Coefficient Derivation:\n\n        `bₙ = \\frac{2}{π} \int_0^{\pi} k \\sin(nx) dx = \\frac{2k}{π} \\left[ (-\\cos(nx) / n) \\right]_0^π = \\frac{2k}{nπ} [1 - (-1)ⁿ]`\n\n        `For even  n: bₙ = 0; \   For odd  n: bₙ = \\frac{4k}{nπ}`\n\n        `f(x) = \\frac{4k}{π} \\left[ \\sin(x) + (\\sin(3x) / 3) + (\\sin(5x) / 5) + (\\sin(7x) / 7) + ... \\right]`\n\n        `Deduction: At point of continuity  x =\\frac{π}{2}, \ f\\frac{π}{2} = k:`\n\n        `k = \\frac{4k}{π} \\left[ \\sin\\frac{π}{2} + (\\sin\\frac{3π}{2} / 3) + (\\sin\\frac{5π}{2} / 5) - ... \\right] = \\frac{4k}{π} \\left[ 1 - \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{5} - \\frac{1}{7} + ... \\right]`\n\n        `⇒ 1 - \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{5} - \\frac{1}{7} + ... = \\frac{π}{4}  (Gregory-Leibniz Series Verified!)`\n```\n\nThe Fourier expansion contains only odd harmonic sine components, rigorously yielding the classical π/4 series identity.\n\n<div class=\"example-answer-box\">\n🎯 **Verified Result:** Question 24 step-by-step resolution per MMDU university marking scheme.\n</div>\n</example>\n\n---"
